00:00:03.990 --> 00:00:10.250
Zoe: The benefit of close working
relationships and partnerships when I'm

00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:14.360
supporting children and young people
with autism is the sort of consistency

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:18.590
across the support that the child
is being offered and ensuring that

00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:22.120
how the people that are working with
them in lots of different settings

00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:25.540
are working with them in the same way
to sort of help their development.

00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:29.800
I also think it's really important in
terms of making decisions and making sure

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:35.080
that the decisions are all being made with
the benefit of the child or young person.

00:00:35.129 --> 00:00:36.810
Ellen: We all work together.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:40.800
We've all got different knowledge
from different backgrounds.

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:44.640
So obviously when you've got a lot
of different people with different

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:49.500
knowledge and you work together,
you know, you help fill the gaps.

00:00:50.099 --> 00:00:57.780
Zoe T: To get real meaningful benefits
with professionals, you need to have

00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:04.860
a shared framework of understanding
about what your goals are and what

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:06.240
your understanding of autism is.

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:14.360
Phil: I think it really has a direct
correlation to the progress, personal

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:19.530
progress, our pupils make, but
also the progress they make in the

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:25.290
classroom because their sensory diet,
their sensory needs are better met.

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:29.699
So rather than just having, as you would
in a mainstream school, a class teacher

00:01:29.729 --> 00:01:33.430
who would differentiate according to
the needs of their pupils, but rather

00:01:33.430 --> 00:01:39.820
just having, sort of,  academic targets,
if you like, there's a wider - the

00:01:39.850 --> 00:01:41.774
wider needs of the learners, are met.

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:45.755
Kerry: It's about getting that specialist
input from other places as well because as

00:01:45.755 --> 00:01:50.495
a teacher, obviously I was trained for the
academic side and the academic support.

00:01:51.682 --> 00:01:53.675
Whereas when I can work with speech
and language therapists, they can

00:01:53.675 --> 00:01:55.265
help me on the communication side.

00:01:56.045 --> 00:01:57.935
An occupational therapist might
be able to help me with the

00:01:57.935 --> 00:01:59.225
self-regulation side of it.

00:01:59.225 --> 00:02:05.250
I think it's really important that we
work with as many teams as possible to get

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:07.759
that overarching support to every level.

00:02:09.050 --> 00:02:10.639
Phil: Well, we follow the teach approach.

00:02:10.639 --> 00:02:15.692
So all the tasks are clearly
labeled -  this is task one, this

00:02:15.692 --> 00:02:19.400
is task two, this is task three, and
then finished or choose activity.

00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:23.990
Visual schedules, so our pupils
always know what they're doing now

00:02:24.079 --> 00:02:26.179
and what's coming next and who with.

00:02:27.025 --> 00:02:30.045
Kerry: It's that holistic approach.

00:02:30.045 --> 00:02:34.785
It's making sure that we are doing
the best for the people we support

00:02:34.875 --> 00:02:37.605
in every area of their life.

